{"id": "gen-1793475698-zf96MM6Rscqtr7Jwp0G9", "object": "chat.completion", "created": 1793475698, "model": "openai/gpt-5.5", "provider": "OpenAI", "system_fingerprint": null, "service_tier": "default", "choices": [{"index": 0, "logprobs": null, "finish_reason": "stop", "native_finish_reason": "completed", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Aluminum is great at moving heat, but that’s not the same as holding a lot of heat.\n\nThink of heat like money. Aluminum is a fast spender: it takes heat from the burner and passes it along quickly to the food. Cast iron is more like a thick savings account. It takes longer to heat up because there’s so much heavy metal to warm, but once it’s hot, it has stored a lot of energy. So when you drop in a cold steak, a handful of mushrooms, or a wet batch of zucchini, the cast-iron pan’s temperature doesn’t crash as fast.\n\nThat’s why a cast-iron skillet can give you better browning on a steak. The meat pulls heat from the pan immediately, and the pan has enough thermal mass to keep searing instead of cooling down and steaming the surface. A thin aluminum pan may heat quickly, but it can lose temperature quickly too, especially if the food is cold or crowded.\n\nOne limitation: this comparison depends on thickness and construction. A thick aluminum pan, especially one with a heavy base, can hold plenty of heat, while a small, thin cast-iron pan won’t feel magically powerful. Conductivity matters; it’s just not the whole story.", "refusal": null, "reasoning_details": [{"type": "reasoning.encrypted", "data": "gAAAAABq5kRzVEKgYzzIxnbPacVtAUzJKmoHQuM2_UZ1RfuWdIUe1NnBWtNteOD6hX5BbA4iEHvRXvPZo6LeRYnDUwwlMsc_VSxmmmlHGiGKQha6qN6I-hgpDRSw2s5Ba9GTQ5_ACx0U7P2zvZ82WZHBVDoz_h1E4oKXBSuTLI6DWt7b-JAyS5JwncLA-sScwLocyhRLUOo3BKLtACScn7eQp6PUa3tqlDqQvykf5PB9osGjTCvxJRpazZaDzgVZS82wGf0up56HcZ7ZAs4psFNmswUJsWyMu9T7xY73LhCJ2WnxlL0AHAShW05J2lR1ASyicQxJt8QCKIbkB61Fv_a86uBYcOoqaGEKTuUvfW_wpBD_e5MPWhZ9CHfJ1S8LcT5OPRNMEb9CcqiDGn_nQdpm8dOsTqxknJZdXSxTp